1
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:13,600
And when we had come down to the ship and the sea,

2
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:17,440
first of all we hauled the ship
down to the bright salt water,

3
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:22,820
and set the mast and the sails in the black ship,
and took the sheep and put them aboard, and ourselves

4
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:26,340
went aboard grieving, letting the big tears fall.

5
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:33,140
And for us, behind the dark-blue-prowed ship,
a following wind that filled the sail, a good companion,

6
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:37,640
was sent by Circe of the lovely braids,
dread goddess with a mortal's voice.

7
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,180
So when we had seen to the tackle through the ship,

8
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:44,040
we sat, and the wind and the steersman held her on course.

9
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,460
All day long her sail was
stretched as she crossed the water;

10
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:50,960
and the sun went down, and all the ways grew dark.

11
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,280
She came to the limits of deep-flowing Ocean.

12
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:59,660
There is the land and city of the Cimmerian men,
shrouded in mist and cloud: never does the shining

13
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:04,300
sun look down on them with his rays,
not when he climbs toward the starry heaven,

14
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:10,280
when he turns back from heaven to earth,
but deadly night is stretched over wretched mortals.

15
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:15,060
Coming there we beached the ship, and took out
the sheep, and walked along the stream of Ocean

16
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:18,220
until we came to the place that Circe had appointed.

17
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:24,320
There Perimedes and Eurylochus held the victims,
and I drew the sharp sword from beside my thigh

18
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:27,240
and dug a pit about a cubit's measure this way and that,

19
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:30,320
and around it I poured a drink-offering to all the dead —

20
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:33,560
first with milk and honey mingled, then with sweet wine,

21
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:37,320
the third time with water;
and I sprinkled white barley over it.

22
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:41,320
And I entreated, over and over,
the strengthless heads of the dead:

23
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:44,180
vowing that, come to Ithaca, I would offer in my halls

24
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:48,700
a barren heifer, my best,
and heap the pyre with good things;

25
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:52,140
and to Tiresias, apart, alone, I would sacrifice a ram

26
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:55,660
all black, the one that stands out from all our flocks.

27
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:58,660
And when with vows and prayers I had entreated them,

28
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:01,620
the tribes of the dead,
I took the sheep and cut their throats

29
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:05,340
over the pit, and the dark-clouded
blood ran; and they gathered,

30
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:08,740
the souls of the dead who are gone, up out of Erebus:

31
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:12,640
brides, and unwed youths,
and old men worn by long suffering,

32
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:18,360
and tender girls with the fresh grief on their hearts,
and many men gashed by the bronze-tipped spears,

33
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:21,600
men killed in war, in armor caked with their blood.

34
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,160
In crowds from every side they came flocking about the pit

35
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:29,600
with a sound that had no earthly likeness;
and the pale terror took me.

36
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,200
Then I roused my companions, and commanded them

37
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:38,140
to flay the sheep that lay there slaughtered by the
pitiless bronze, and burn them, and pray to the gods —

38
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:44,030
to mighty Hades and dread Persephone; and I myself,
drawing the sharp sword from beside my thigh,

39
00:02:44,030 --> 00:02:47,110
sat there, and would not let the
strengthless heads of the dead

40
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:50,570
come near the blood before I had questioned Tiresias.

41
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:57,150
First came the soul of my companion Elpenor,
for he was not yet buried under the wide-wayed earth:

42
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:04,610
we had left his body behind in Circe's hall,
unwept and unburied, since another task was driving us.

43
00:03:04,610 --> 00:03:10,750
I wept to see him, and my heart pitied him,
and lifting my voice I spoke winged words to him:

44
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:14,290
'Elpenor — how did you come here, under the misty dark?

45
00:03:14,290 --> 00:03:18,270
You have outstripped me —
on foot, and I in my black ship.'

46
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:21,830
So I spoke, and he groaned aloud, and answered:

47
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:25,670
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices,

48
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:29,890
the evil doom of a god blinded me —
that, and wine past measure.

49
00:03:29,890 --> 00:03:34,990
Lying down to sleep in Circe's hall, I never
thought to come back down again by the long ladder;

50
00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:40,770
I fell headlong from the roof, and my neck snapped
from the spine; and my soul came down to Hades.

51
00:03:40,770 --> 00:03:44,770
Now I beg you — by those left behind,
those not here beside us:

52
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:48,630
by your wife; by your father,
who reared you when you were small;

53
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:51,990
by Telemachus, whom you left an only child in your halls.

54
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:55,410
For I know that when you go from here,
from the house of Hades,

55
00:03:55,410 --> 00:03:58,830
you will put in again with your
well-made ship at the island of Aeaea:

56
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:01,710
there, my lord, I charge you: remember me.

57
00:04:01,710 --> 00:04:04,950
Do not go on and leave me behind you unwept, unburied,

58
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:08,750
turning away — lest I become a
cause of gods' wrath against you —

59
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:13,770
but burn me with all the armor that is mine,
and heap me a mound on the shore of the gray salt sea:

60
00:04:13,770 --> 00:04:16,870
an unlucky man's marker, for men to come to learn of.

61
00:04:16,870 --> 00:04:19,690
Accomplish this for me — and fix upon the mound the oar

62
00:04:19,690 --> 00:04:23,190
I rowed with when I lived,
when I was among my companions.'

63
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:26,390
So he spoke, and I answered him and said:

64
00:04:26,390 --> 00:04:29,890
'All this, poor luckless man, I will accomplish and do.'

65
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:35,170
So the two of us sat trading grim words,
I on one side, holding my sword over the blood,

66
00:04:35,170 --> 00:04:39,170
and across from me the phantom
of my companion, talking on.

67
00:04:39,170 --> 00:04:44,770
Then came the soul of my dead mother,
Anticleia, daughter of great-hearted Autolycus,

68
00:04:44,770 --> 00:04:47,750
whom I had left alive when I went to holy Ilium.

69
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:53,350
I wept to see her, and my heart pitied her;
but even so, for all my thick grief, I would not let her

70
00:04:53,350 --> 00:04:57,050
come near the blood before I had questioned Tiresias.

71
00:04:57,050 --> 00:05:02,970
Then came the soul of Theban Tiresias,
holding a golden scepter; and he knew me, and spoke:

72
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:06,710
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —

73
00:05:06,710 --> 00:05:09,990
what now, unlucky man?
Why have you left the light of the sun

74
00:05:09,990 --> 00:05:13,490
and come to see the dead, and this place without joy?

75
00:05:13,490 --> 00:05:16,230
Draw back from the pit; hold off your sharp sword,

76
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:20,190
so that I may drink of the blood,
and tell you the unerring truth.'

77
00:05:20,190 --> 00:05:23,830
So he spoke, and I stepped back,
and drove the silver-studded sword

78
00:05:23,830 --> 00:05:30,290
home in its sheath. And when he had drunk the dark blood,
then at last the blameless seer addressed me:

79
00:05:30,290 --> 00:05:33,730
'It is honey-sweet homecoming you seek, shining Odysseus,

80
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:36,670
and a god will make it grim for you. For I do not think

81
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:41,130
the shaker of earth will fail to see you —
the grudge is set in his heart,

82
00:05:41,130 --> 00:05:43,550
raging because you blinded his dear son.

83
00:05:43,550 --> 00:05:47,110
Yet even so, though you suffer evils,
you may still come home,

84
00:05:47,110 --> 00:05:51,030
if you are willing to hold hard
your own heart and your companions',

85
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:56,910
from the hour when you first bring your well-made ship
in to the island Thrinacia, escaping the violet sea,

86
00:05:56,910 --> 00:05:59,770
and find at pasture there the cattle and the strong flocks

87
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:03,370
of Helios, who sees all things and hears all things.

88
00:06:03,370 --> 00:06:06,690
If you leave them unharmed,
and keep your mind on homecoming,

89
00:06:06,690 --> 00:06:10,110
then you may yet reach Ithaca, though you suffer evils.

90
00:06:10,110 --> 00:06:16,270
But if you harm them, then I foretell destruction for
ship and companions; and even if you yourself escape,

91
00:06:16,270 --> 00:06:19,790
late you will come, and in evil case, all companions lost,

92
00:06:19,790 --> 00:06:25,690
on another man's ship; and you will find trouble in
your house — overweening men, who eat away your living,

93
00:06:25,690 --> 00:06:28,890
courting your godlike wife and offering bride-gifts.

94
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:32,050
Yet, truly, you will come and repay their violence.

95
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:37,590
And when you have killed the suitors in your halls,
whether by guile or in the open, with the sharp bronze,

96
00:06:37,590 --> 00:06:43,110
then go on a journey, taking a well-shaped oar,
until you come to men who know nothing of the sea,

97
00:06:43,110 --> 00:06:47,590
men who eat no food that is mixed with salt,
who know nothing of red-cheeked ships,

98
00:06:47,590 --> 00:06:50,870
nothing of well-shaped oars, which are wings for ships.

99
00:06:50,870 --> 00:06:55,030
And I will tell you a sign,
unmistakable: it will not escape you.

100
00:06:55,030 --> 00:07:00,350
When another traveler, meeting you on the road,
says you carry a winnowing-fan on your shining shoulder,

101
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:06,070
then and there fix the well-shaped oar in the
earth and offer fine victims to the lord Poseidon —

102
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:11,770
a ram, a bull, and a boar that has mounted sows —
and turn away home, and offer holy hecatombs

103
00:07:11,770 --> 00:07:17,550
to the deathless gods who hold wide heaven,
to all of them, each in order. And death will come to you

104
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:23,650
from the sea, so gentle a death, and take you worn
down under sleek old age; and around you the peoples

105
00:07:23,650 --> 00:07:27,190
will be prosperous. This I tell you is unerring truth.'

106
00:07:27,190 --> 00:07:29,990
So he spoke, and I answered him and said:

107
00:07:29,990 --> 00:07:33,670
'Tiresias — all that, no doubt,
the gods themselves have spun.

108
00:07:33,670 --> 00:07:36,510
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly.

109
00:07:36,510 --> 00:07:38,650
I see here the soul of my dead mother:

110
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:41,470
she sits in silence near the blood,
and cannot bring herself

111
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:43,850
to look her own son in the face.

112
00:07:43,850 --> 00:07:47,770
Tell me, lord: how may she know me for the man I am?'

113
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:50,330
So I spoke, and he answered me at once:

114
00:07:50,330 --> 00:07:53,470
'An easy word I will tell you, and set it in your mind.

115
00:07:53,470 --> 00:07:58,290
Whomever of the dead who are gone you allow to
come near the blood, that one will speak you truth;

116
00:07:58,290 --> 00:08:01,470
whomever you begrudge it, that one will draw back and go.'

117
00:08:01,470 --> 00:08:04,450
So speaking, the soul of the lord Tiresias went back

118
00:08:04,450 --> 00:08:07,990
into the house of Hades, when he
had told out the decrees of fate.

119
00:08:07,990 --> 00:08:10,290
But I stayed rooted where I was until my mother

120
00:08:10,290 --> 00:08:16,930
came and drank the dark-clouded blood. And at once
she knew me, and wailing she spoke winged words to me:

121
00:08:16,930 --> 00:08:19,890
'My child — how did you come here, under the misty dark,

122
00:08:19,890 --> 00:08:23,650
alive? Hard it is for the living to look on all this:

123
00:08:23,650 --> 00:08:30,650
for between lie great rivers, and terrible waters —
Ocean first of all, which there is no crossing on foot,

124
00:08:30,650 --> 00:08:32,410
never, unless a man has a well-made ship.

125
00:08:32,410 --> 00:08:35,410
Are you only now come wandering here from Troy —

126
00:08:35,410 --> 00:08:39,950
after all this time — with ship
and companions? Have you not yet

127
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:43,690
been to Ithaca — not seen your wife in your own halls?'

128
00:08:43,690 --> 00:08:46,790
So she spoke, and I answered her and said:

129
00:08:46,790 --> 00:08:49,050
'Mother, need brought me down to Hades:

130
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:51,690
to consult the soul of Theban Tiresias.

131
00:08:51,690 --> 00:08:54,250
For not yet have I come near Achaea — not yet

132
00:08:54,250 --> 00:08:58,790
set foot on our own land;
I have wandered without pause, in misery,

133
00:08:58,790 --> 00:09:05,210
since the day I first followed noble Agamemnon toward
Ilium of the fine horses, to fight the Trojans.

134
00:09:05,210 --> 00:09:08,050
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

135
00:09:08,050 --> 00:09:11,150
what doom of death, that lays men low, mastered you?

136
00:09:11,150 --> 00:09:16,430
A long illness? Or did Artemis the arrow-pourer
come with her gentle arrows, and kill you?

137
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:19,410
And tell me of my father, and the son I left behind:

138
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:22,110
does my honor still rest safe
with them, or does some other

139
00:09:22,110 --> 00:09:25,850
man hold it now — do they say I will come home no more?

140
00:09:25,850 --> 00:09:28,490
And tell me the mind and purpose of my wedded wife:

141
00:09:28,490 --> 00:09:31,570
does she stay by the boy and keep all things safe?

142
00:09:31,570 --> 00:09:34,810
Or has the best man of the Achaeans married her already?'

143
00:09:34,810 --> 00:09:38,230
So I spoke, and at once my honored mother answered:

144
00:09:38,230 --> 00:09:43,990
'Oh yes — she stays, and more: with an enduring heart
she is there in your halls; and always in misery

145
00:09:43,990 --> 00:09:47,630
her nights waste away, and her days, in tears.

146
00:09:47,630 --> 00:09:53,450
Your fine honor no man holds yet: at his ease
Telemachus farms the estates unchallenged, and feasts

147
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:57,270
at the equal banquets it is fitting
a lawgiving man should tend:

148
00:09:57,270 --> 00:10:00,650
for all men issue him the summons.
But your father stays out there

149
00:10:00,650 --> 00:10:03,830
on the farm, and never comes down to the city. Bedding

150
00:10:03,830 --> 00:10:08,190
he has none — no bedstead, no cloaks, no shining blankets:

151
00:10:08,190 --> 00:10:11,050
through the winter he sleeps
where the slaves do, in the house,

152
00:10:11,050 --> 00:10:17,230
in the ashes by the fire, and puts poor clothing on
his skin; but when summer comes, and the heavy autumn,

153
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:22,590
then anywhere along the rise of his vineyard plot a
bed of fallen leaves is thrown on the ground for him,

154
00:10:22,590 --> 00:10:26,470
and there he lies grieving,
and the sorrow grows great in his heart,

155
00:10:26,470 --> 00:10:30,130
aching for your homecoming; and hard old age comes on him.

156
00:10:30,130 --> 00:10:34,270
And so it was with me: so I too died and met my doom.

157
00:10:34,270 --> 00:10:38,730
Not in the halls did the keen-eyed arrow-pourer
come with her gentle arrows and kill me;

158
00:10:38,730 --> 00:10:44,470
no illness fell upon me, of the kind that most often
draws the life from the limbs in hateful wasting:

159
00:10:44,470 --> 00:10:48,490
no — it was longing for you,
your counsels, shining Odysseus,

160
00:10:48,490 --> 00:10:51,670
your gentle-heartedness,
that robbed me of honey-sweet life.'

161
00:10:51,670 --> 00:10:57,590
So she spoke; and I, my mind stirred,
longed to take the soul of my dead mother in my arms.

162
00:10:57,590 --> 00:11:01,430
Three times I started toward her,
and my heart bade me hold her;

163
00:11:01,430 --> 00:11:04,530
three times, out of my hands, like a shadow or a dream

164
00:11:04,530 --> 00:11:08,350
she flew. And the grief grew
sharper in my heart each time,

165
00:11:08,350 --> 00:11:11,130
and lifting my voice I spoke winged words to her:

166
00:11:11,130 --> 00:11:15,090
'Mother — why will you not stay
for me, when I long to hold you,

167
00:11:15,090 --> 00:11:18,830
so that even here in Hades,
throwing our arms around each other,

168
00:11:18,830 --> 00:11:21,530
the two of us might take our fill of icy mourning?

169
00:11:21,530 --> 00:11:27,030
Or is this a phantom queenly Persephone has sent me,
so that I may weep and groan the more?'

170
00:11:27,030 --> 00:11:30,470
So I spoke, and at once my honored mother answered:

171
00:11:30,470 --> 00:11:33,950
'Ah me, my child, luckless beyond all mortal men —

172
00:11:33,950 --> 00:11:37,250
in no way does Persephone,
the daughter of Zeus, deceive you:

173
00:11:37,250 --> 00:11:40,590
this is the settled way of mortals, when one dies.

174
00:11:40,590 --> 00:11:43,810
The sinews no longer hold the flesh and bones together:

175
00:11:43,810 --> 00:11:50,090
all that, the strong fury of the blazing fire breaks down,
as soon as the life has left the white bones,

176
00:11:50,090 --> 00:11:53,550
and the soul flits away like a dream, and hovers.

177
00:11:53,550 --> 00:11:57,150
But press on toward the light,
as fast as you can. And mark

178
00:11:57,150 --> 00:12:01,690
all these things — to tell them to your wife hereafter.'

179
00:12:01,690 --> 00:12:08,190
So the two of us traded words. And then the women
came — for queenly Persephone sent them on —

180
00:12:08,190 --> 00:12:10,990
all who had been the wives and daughters of the great.

181
00:12:10,990 --> 00:12:17,170
They came flocking in crowds around the dark blood,
and I considered how I might question each in turn.

182
00:12:17,170 --> 00:12:21,050
And this, as I turned it over, seemed to me the best plan:

183
00:12:21,050 --> 00:12:24,370
drawing the long-edged sword from beside my thick thigh,

184
00:12:24,370 --> 00:12:27,090
I would not let them drink the dark blood all together.

185
00:12:27,090 --> 00:12:33,610
So they came up one by one, in file, and each
declared her lineage; and I questioned them all.

186
00:12:33,610 --> 00:12:40,270
There the first I saw was Tyro, born of a noble father,
who said she was the child of blameless Salmoneus,

187
00:12:40,270 --> 00:12:44,150
and called herself the wife of Cretheus, Aeolus' son.

188
00:12:44,150 --> 00:12:50,090
She fell in love with a river — divine Enipeus,
far the most beautiful of rivers flowing on the earth —

189
00:12:50,090 --> 00:12:52,890
and she would haunt Enipeus' beautiful streams.

190
00:12:52,890 --> 00:12:56,250
And the earth-holder, the shaker
of earth, took the river's likeness

191
00:12:56,250 --> 00:12:59,530
and lay beside her at the mouths of the eddying water;

192
00:12:59,530 --> 00:13:03,250
and a dark-gleaming wave stood
up around them, mountain-high,

193
00:13:03,250 --> 00:13:06,070
arched over, and hid the god and the mortal woman.

194
00:13:06,070 --> 00:13:09,690
He loosed the girdle of her maidenhood,
and poured sleep over her.

195
00:13:09,690 --> 00:13:16,030
And when the god had finished the works of love,
he clamped a hand on hers, and spoke a word, and named her:

196
00:13:16,030 --> 00:13:18,970
'Rejoice in this love, woman. As the year turns round

197
00:13:18,970 --> 00:13:25,210
you will bear glorious children — for the beds of the
immortals are not fruitless. Tend them, and rear them.

198
00:13:25,210 --> 00:13:28,710
Now go home, and hold your peace, and name no name.

199
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:31,730
But know it: I am Poseidon, shaker of the earth.'

200
00:13:31,730 --> 00:13:34,350
So speaking he plunged under the surging sea.

201
00:13:34,350 --> 00:13:40,530
And she conceived, and bore Pelias and Neleus,
who both grew to be strong servants of great Zeus;

202
00:13:40,530 --> 00:13:46,830
Pelias lived in wide-dancing-grounded Iolcus,
rich in flocks, and Neleus in sandy Pylos.

203
00:13:46,830 --> 00:13:50,250
And her other sons this queen
among women bore to Cretheus:

204
00:13:50,250 --> 00:13:53,630
Aeson, and Pheres, and Amythaon the chariot-fighter.

205
00:13:53,630 --> 00:14:00,050
After her I saw Antiope, Asopus' daughter,
who could boast she had slept in the very arms of Zeus,

206
00:14:00,050 --> 00:14:05,910
and she bore two sons, Amphion and Zethus,
who first founded the seat of seven-gated Thebes

207
00:14:05,910 --> 00:14:08,970
and walled it round — for without walls they could not

208
00:14:08,970 --> 00:14:12,830
live in wide-dancing-grounded Thebes,
strong though they were.

209
00:14:12,830 --> 00:14:19,910
After her I saw Alcmene, Amphitryon's wife,
who bore Heracles, the lion-hearted, bold in the fight,

210
00:14:19,910 --> 00:14:25,650
after she lay mingled in the arms of great Zeus;
and Megara, daughter of high-spirited Creon,

211
00:14:25,650 --> 00:14:30,490
whom Amphitryon's son held as wife —
the man whose strength never wore.

212
00:14:30,490 --> 00:14:36,890
And I saw the mother of Oedipus, beautiful Epicaste,
who did a monstrous thing in the ignorance of her mind,

213
00:14:36,890 --> 00:14:40,610
marrying her own son; and he, who had killed his father,

214
00:14:40,610 --> 00:14:44,070
married her; and the gods made it known among men at once.

215
00:14:44,070 --> 00:14:50,190
Yet he ruled on over the Cadmeians in lovely Thebes,
suffering pains, through the gods' deadly designs;

216
00:14:50,190 --> 00:14:54,180
and she went down to the house of
Hades, strong warden of the gate,

217
00:14:54,180 --> 00:14:56,930
fastening a noose to hang sheer from the high roof-beam,

218
00:14:56,930 --> 00:14:59,990
gripped by her anguish — and left behind for him the pains

219
00:14:59,990 --> 00:15:02,970
past counting that a mother's Furies bring to pass.

220
00:15:02,970 --> 00:15:07,470
And I saw Chloris, beautiful
past telling, whom Neleus once

221
00:15:07,470 --> 00:15:11,170
married for her beauty, bringing bride-gifts past number:

222
00:15:11,170 --> 00:15:17,190
youngest daughter of Amphion, Iasus' son,
who once ruled in strength in Minyan Orchomenus.

223
00:15:17,190 --> 00:15:20,430
She was queen of Pylos, and bore him shining children:

224
00:15:20,430 --> 00:15:27,230
Nestor, and Chromius, and lordly Periclymenus,
and after them she bore that wonder of mortals, strong Pero,

225
00:15:27,230 --> 00:15:32,310
whom all the neighboring men were courting;
but Neleus would give her to no man who could not drive off

226
00:15:32,310 --> 00:15:35,390
the crook-horned, broad-browed cattle of mighty Iphicles

227
00:15:35,390 --> 00:15:39,310
out of Phylace — a dangerous herd. Only the blameless seer

228
00:15:39,310 --> 00:15:43,450
undertook to drive them; and the
hard doom of a god bound him fast,

229
00:15:43,450 --> 00:15:46,570
the cruel bonds, and the herdsmen of the wild.

230
00:15:46,570 --> 00:15:50,090
But when at last the months and the
days were coming to fulfillment,

231
00:15:50,090 --> 00:15:55,470
as the year rolled round and the seasons came again,
then at last mighty Iphicles set him free,

232
00:15:55,470 --> 00:16:00,990
when he had told out all the decrees of fate.
And the design of Zeus was accomplished.

233
00:16:00,990 --> 00:16:06,390
And I saw Leda, wife of Tyndareus,
who bore to Tyndareus two strong-hearted sons:

234
00:16:06,390 --> 00:16:10,150
Castor the horse-breaker,
and Polydeuces good with his fists.

235
00:16:10,150 --> 00:16:13,630
These two the life-giving earth
holds — both of them living:

236
00:16:13,630 --> 00:16:18,710
even under the earth they have honor from Zeus,
and live turn about, one day each in turn,

237
00:16:18,710 --> 00:16:22,770
and each in turn are dead.
They have won honor equal to the gods'.

238
00:16:22,770 --> 00:16:27,850
After her I saw Iphimedeia, wife of Aloeus,
who could say that she had lain with Poseidon,

239
00:16:27,850 --> 00:16:34,030
and she bore two sons — but short-lived they proved —
godlike Otus, and far-famed Ephialtes:

240
00:16:34,030 --> 00:16:40,070
the tallest men the grain-giving earth ever nourished,
and far the most beautiful, after famous Orion.

241
00:16:40,070 --> 00:16:45,040
At nine years old they measured nine
cubits across, and stood nine fathoms high;

242
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:48,650
To carry the din of furious war up into Olympus.

243
00:16:48,650 --> 00:16:55,080
Ossa they strained to pile on Olympus, and on Ossa
Pelion of the shivering leaves, to make heaven scalable.

244
00:16:55,080 --> 00:16:58,610
And they would have accomplished it,
had they reached the measure of manhood;

245
00:16:58,610 --> 00:17:02,250
but the son of Zeus,
whom fair-haired Leto bore, destroyed them

246
00:17:02,250 --> 00:17:05,770
both, before the down had bloomed beneath their temples

247
00:17:05,770 --> 00:17:09,230
and thickened on their chins
in the flower of a young beard.

248
00:17:09,230 --> 00:17:12,990
And Phaedra I saw, and Procris, and beautiful Ariadne,

249
00:17:12,990 --> 00:17:16,190
the daughter of Minos the deadly-minded, whom Theseus once

250
00:17:16,190 --> 00:17:18,910
carried off from Crete, making for the hill of holy

251
00:17:18,910 --> 00:17:26,610
Athens — and had no joy of her: Artemis killed her first,
on sea-ringed Dia, on the testimony of Dionysus.

252
00:17:26,610 --> 00:17:33,270
And Maera I saw, and Clymene, and hateful Eriphyle,
who took precious gold as the price of her own husband.

253
00:17:33,270 --> 00:17:38,850
But all of them I could not tell through or name —
all the wives and daughters of heroes I saw there:

254
00:17:38,850 --> 00:17:42,410
the divine night would waste
away first. No — it is the hour

255
00:17:42,410 --> 00:17:45,550
for sleep, whether I go down to the fast ship and the crew

256
00:17:45,550 --> 00:17:50,110
or stay here. My sending rests
with the gods, and with you."

257
00:17:50,110 --> 00:17:56,150
So he spoke; and they were all hushed in silence,
held fast under the spell, through the shadowy halls.

258
00:17:56,150 --> 00:17:59,430
And white-armed Arete began to speak among them:

259
00:17:59,430 --> 00:18:02,290
"Phaeacians — how does this man appear to you now:

260
00:18:02,290 --> 00:18:06,170
his looks, his stature, the balanced mind within?

261
00:18:06,170 --> 00:18:09,510
And he is my guest — though each of you shares the honor.

262
00:18:09,510 --> 00:18:11,950
Do not hurry, then, to send him off, and do not cut short

263
00:18:11,950 --> 00:18:17,590
your gifts to one so needy: for many are the
treasures lying in your halls by the gods' good will."

264
00:18:17,590 --> 00:18:23,290
And among them spoke the old hero Echeneus,
the man elder-born beyond the other Phaeacians:

265
00:18:23,290 --> 00:18:26,050
"Friends — not wide of the mark,
not short of our own thought,

266
00:18:26,050 --> 00:18:29,170
are the words of our watchful-minded queen. Heed them.

267
00:18:29,170 --> 00:18:32,450
But on Alcinous here depend both word and deed."

268
00:18:32,450 --> 00:18:35,130
Then Alcinous answered him in turn, and said:

269
00:18:35,130 --> 00:18:40,170
"This word shall stand — so surely as I
live and rule the oar-loving Phaeacians.

270
00:18:40,170 --> 00:18:43,550
Let the guest endure it, then,
for all his hunger for homecoming:

271
00:18:43,550 --> 00:18:49,890
let him stay till tomorrow, until I make our whole
gift complete. His sending shall be the men's concern —

272
00:18:49,890 --> 00:18:53,830
all men's, and mine above all:
for mine is the power in this land.

273
00:18:53,830 --> 00:18:56,530
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

274
00:18:56,530 --> 00:19:01,370
"Lord Alcinous, most renowned among all peoples,
if you bade me stay here even for a year

275
00:19:01,370 --> 00:19:07,350
and readied my sending, and gave me shining gifts,
even that I would choose; and it would profit me far more

276
00:19:07,350 --> 00:19:10,030
to come to my own dear country with a fuller hand:

277
00:19:10,030 --> 00:19:15,290
so should I be more honored, and more dear,
to all the men who saw me sailing home to Ithaca."

278
00:19:15,290 --> 00:19:18,050
Then Alcinous answered him in turn, and said:

279
00:19:18,050 --> 00:19:23,350
"Odysseus — looking at you, we cannot liken you to
a cheat or a thief — the kind the black earth breeds

280
00:19:23,350 --> 00:19:25,630
in such crops, scattered everywhere:

281
00:19:25,630 --> 00:19:28,590
men who build lies out of what no eye could ever test.

282
00:19:28,590 --> 00:19:32,090
There is a shape upon your words,
and a noble mind within them,

283
00:19:32,090 --> 00:19:35,070
and you have told your tale
with knowledge, like a singer —

284
00:19:35,070 --> 00:19:37,990
the grim sorrows of all the Argives, and your own.

285
00:19:37,990 --> 00:19:40,390
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

286
00:19:40,390 --> 00:19:46,310
did you see any of your godlike companions,
who went to Ilium with you, and met their doom there?

287
00:19:46,310 --> 00:19:52,690
This night is long — marvelously long. It is not yet
time to sleep in the hall. Tell me the deeds of wonder.

288
00:19:52,690 --> 00:19:58,170
I could hold out until the shining dawn, if only you
could bear, here in the hall, to tell me your sorrows."

289
00:19:58,170 --> 00:20:00,750
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

290
00:20:00,750 --> 00:20:05,910
"Lord Alcinous, most renowned among all peoples —
there is an hour for many tales, and an hour for sleep.

291
00:20:05,910 --> 00:20:11,190
But if you long to listen still, I would not grudge
you the telling of things more pitiful still:

292
00:20:11,190 --> 00:20:14,540
the sorrows of my companions, who perished afterward —

293
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:16,470
Battle-cry and its groaning

294
00:20:16,470 --> 00:20:20,550
only to die on the homeward way, by an evil woman's will.

295
00:20:20,550 --> 00:20:25,230
Now when holy Persephone had scattered
the women's souls this way and that,

296
00:20:25,230 --> 00:20:31,630
there came the soul of Agamemnon, Atreus' son,
grieving; and around him others were gathered — all who

297
00:20:31,630 --> 00:20:35,510
died with him in the house of
Aegisthus, and met their doom.

298
00:20:35,510 --> 00:20:38,630
He knew me at once, when he had drunk the dark blood,

299
00:20:38,630 --> 00:20:42,710
and he wept, shrill and loud, letting the big tears fall,

300
00:20:42,710 --> 00:20:45,850
stretching his hands toward me, straining to reach me —

301
00:20:45,850 --> 00:20:51,970
but there was no strength grounded in him now,
no force such as once filled his supple limbs.

302
00:20:51,970 --> 00:20:58,130
I wept to see him, and my heart pitied him,
and lifting my voice I spoke winged words to him:

303
00:20:58,130 --> 00:21:05,210
'Most glorious son of Atreus, Agamemnon, lord of men —
what doom of death, that lays men low, mastered you?

304
00:21:05,210 --> 00:21:09,530
Did Poseidon master you in your ships,
rousing the wretched blast of hard winds?

305
00:21:09,530 --> 00:21:15,510
Or did hostile men cut you down on dry land as you
carved away their cattle and their fine flocks of sheep,

306
00:21:15,510 --> 00:21:17,850
or fought them for their city and their women?'

307
00:21:17,850 --> 00:21:20,570
So I spoke, and he answered me at once:

308
00:21:20,570 --> 00:21:24,410
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices:

309
00:21:24,410 --> 00:21:28,890
Poseidon did not master me in my ships,
rousing the wretched blast of hard winds,

310
00:21:28,890 --> 00:21:32,090
and hostile men did not cut me down on dry land.

311
00:21:32,090 --> 00:21:38,210
Aegisthus built my death and my doom, and killed me —
with my cursed wife — after calling me to his house

312
00:21:38,210 --> 00:21:41,930
and feasting me: as a man cuts down an ox at the manger.

313
00:21:41,930 --> 00:21:45,490
So I died the most pitiable of
deaths; and around me my other

314
00:21:45,490 --> 00:21:48,550
companions were killed without pause, like white-tusked

315
00:21:48,550 --> 00:21:51,230
swine slaughtered in the house of a rich and powerful man

316
00:21:51,230 --> 00:21:54,550
for a wedding, a club-feast, or a swelling banquet.

317
00:21:54,550 --> 00:22:00,070
You have stood in the killing of many men before now,
cut down singly, or in the crush of battle —

318
00:22:00,070 --> 00:22:03,490
but you would have grieved at
heart above all to see that sight:

319
00:22:03,490 --> 00:22:09,670
how around the wine-bowl and the loaded tables we lay
in the hall, and the whole floor steamed with blood.

320
00:22:09,670 --> 00:22:12,870
And the most pitiful voice I
heard was Priam's daughter's —

321
00:22:12,870 --> 00:22:15,970
Cassandra's, whom Clytemnestra
of the crooked counsels killed

322
00:22:15,970 --> 00:22:19,490
over my body; and I, dying, tried to raise my hands

323
00:22:19,490 --> 00:22:22,990
and beat them down on the sword-blade —
and she, the bitch-faced,

324
00:22:22,990 --> 00:22:27,250
turned her back: she could not bring herself,
though I was going down to Hades,

325
00:22:27,250 --> 00:22:31,170
to draw my eyelids shut with her hands, or close my mouth.

326
00:22:31,170 --> 00:22:35,410
So true it is that nothing is more dread,
more dog-shameless, than a woman

327
00:22:35,410 --> 00:22:40,770
who can set her mind on deeds like these —
the way that woman plotted her unspeakable work,

328
00:22:40,770 --> 00:22:43,950
building the murder of her own
wedded husband. And I had thought

329
00:22:43,950 --> 00:22:46,850
to come home welcome to my children and my slaves!

330
00:22:46,850 --> 00:22:52,490
But she — that mistress of atrocity — has poured out
shame on her own head, and on all women after her,

331
00:22:52,490 --> 00:22:56,250
the female kind — yes, even on the doer of good.'

332
00:22:56,250 --> 00:22:59,150
So he spoke, and I answered him and said:

333
00:22:59,150 --> 00:23:02,370
'Ah, gods — how terribly has Zeus of the wide voice hated

334
00:23:02,370 --> 00:23:05,230
the seed of Atreus from the beginning,
and through the designs

335
00:23:05,230 --> 00:23:08,850
of women: for Helen's sake we died in our thousands,

336
00:23:08,850 --> 00:23:12,930
and for you Clytemnestra set her
snare while you were far away.'

337
00:23:12,930 --> 00:23:15,410
So I spoke, and he answered me at once:

338
00:23:15,410 --> 00:23:18,490
'Then never be gentle, you too, even to your own wife.

339
00:23:18,490 --> 00:23:21,290
Do not tell out to her the whole word that you know:

340
00:23:21,290 --> 00:23:23,330
say part, and let part stay hidden.

341
00:23:23,330 --> 00:23:26,970
But not for you, Odysseus, will murder come from a wife.

342
00:23:26,970 --> 00:23:30,670
Too understanding she is,
too watchful the mind in her breast —

343
00:23:30,670 --> 00:23:34,090
the daughter of Icarius, watchful-minded Penelope.

344
00:23:34,090 --> 00:23:38,290
A young bride we left her, when we went
to the war; and a boy was at her breast,

345
00:23:38,290 --> 00:23:42,010
an infant, who now, I suppose, sits numbered among men —

346
00:23:42,010 --> 00:23:45,550
the fortunate one! His dear
father will see him, coming home,

347
00:23:45,550 --> 00:23:49,210
and he will embrace his father:
that is the right way of things.

348
00:23:49,210 --> 00:23:51,690
But my wife did not let me fill even my eyes

349
00:23:51,690 --> 00:23:56,050
with my own son. She killed me
before that — killed the man himself.

350
00:23:56,050 --> 00:23:59,110
And I will tell you another thing:
lay it up in your heart.

351
00:23:59,110 --> 00:24:02,370
In secret — not in the open — bring your ship in

352
00:24:02,370 --> 00:24:06,650
to your own dear country.
For there is no trusting women any more.

353
00:24:06,650 --> 00:24:11,470
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

354
00:24:11,470 --> 00:24:17,570
do you hear whether my son is still alive,
somewhere — in Orchomenus perhaps, or in sandy Pylos,

355
00:24:17,570 --> 00:24:20,390
or with Menelaus in wide Sparta?

356
00:24:20,390 --> 00:24:24,110
For noble Orestes has not yet died on the earth.'

357
00:24:24,110 --> 00:24:27,410
So he spoke, and I answered him and said:

358
00:24:27,410 --> 00:24:30,870
'Son of Atreus, why ask me this? I know nothing —

359
00:24:30,870 --> 00:24:35,790
whether he lives or is dead.
It is evil to speak words empty as wind.'

360
00:24:35,790 --> 00:24:41,750
So the two of us stood trading grim words,
grieving, letting the big tears fall.

361
00:24:41,750 --> 00:24:48,010
And there came the soul of Achilles, Peleus' son,
and of Patroclus, and of blameless Antilochus,

362
00:24:48,010 --> 00:24:54,990
and of Ajax, who was first in beauty and in body of
all the Danaans, after the blameless son of Peleus.

363
00:24:54,990 --> 00:25:01,070
And the soul of swift-footed Aeacides knew me,
and mourning he spoke winged words to me:

364
00:25:01,070 --> 00:25:04,830
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —

365
00:25:04,830 --> 00:25:09,090
reckless man! What deed will your
mind plot now, still greater?

366
00:25:09,090 --> 00:25:15,910
How did you dare come down to Hades, where the dead
live without wits — the phantoms of worn-out mortals?'

367
00:25:15,910 --> 00:25:18,590
So he spoke, and I answered him and said:

368
00:25:18,590 --> 00:25:22,120
'Achilles, son of Peleus,
far the mightiest of the Achaeans —

369
00:25:22,120 --> 00:25:28,210
I came for Tiresias, out of need: in hope he
might tell some plan for reaching rugged Ithaca.

370
00:25:28,210 --> 00:25:30,810
For not yet have I come near Achaea — not yet

371
00:25:30,810 --> 00:25:35,630
set foot on my own land: evil is
with me always. But you, Achilles —

372
00:25:35,630 --> 00:25:39,210
no man before you was more blessed,
and none will be after:

373
00:25:39,210 --> 00:25:44,110
living, we Argives honored you like the gods,
and now you rule in strength among the dead,

374
00:25:44,110 --> 00:25:47,990
here where you are.
Do not grieve at dying, then, Achilles.'

375
00:25:47,990 --> 00:25:50,290
So I spoke, and he answered me at once:

376
00:25:50,290 --> 00:25:53,990
'Do not talk death over sweetly to me, shining Odysseus.

377
00:25:53,990 --> 00:25:57,530
I would rather be bound to the
plow-land, hired out to another —

378
00:25:57,530 --> 00:26:02,450
some man without a land-lot, without much living —
than be king over all the wasted dead.

379
00:26:02,450 --> 00:26:05,310
Enough — tell me word of that proud son of mine:

380
00:26:05,310 --> 00:26:08,650
did he follow to the war, to be a champion, or did he not?

381
00:26:08,650 --> 00:26:11,250
And tell me of blameless Peleus, if you have heard:

382
00:26:11,250 --> 00:26:16,790
does he still hold his honor among the Myrmidon crowds,
or do they slight him now through Hellas and Phthia,

383
00:26:16,790 --> 00:26:19,870
because old age has hold of him, hand and foot?

384
00:26:19,870 --> 00:26:25,590
For I am no longer his defender under the rays of
the sun — no longer the man I was when in wide Troy

385
00:26:25,590 --> 00:26:29,010
I cut down the best of their host,
in the Argives' defense.

386
00:26:29,010 --> 00:26:32,150
Could I come, that man,
even for an hour, to my father's house,

387
00:26:32,150 --> 00:26:34,970
I would make my fury and my unconquerable hands a horror

388
00:26:34,970 --> 00:26:38,250
to those who do him violence and shut him from his honor.'

389
00:26:38,250 --> 00:26:40,970
So he spoke, and I answered him and said:

390
00:26:40,970 --> 00:26:42,670
'Of blameless Peleus I have heard nothing.

391
00:26:42,670 --> 00:26:48,070
But of your own dear son, Neoptolemus,
I will tell you the whole truth, as you ask.

392
00:26:48,070 --> 00:26:54,010
I myself carried him, in my trim hollow ship,
out of Scyros to join the strong-greaved Achaeans.

393
00:26:54,010 --> 00:26:57,170
And whenever we framed our
counsels around the city of Troy,

394
00:26:57,170 --> 00:27:00,630
he was always first to speak, and his words never missed:

395
00:27:00,630 --> 00:27:03,090
godlike Nestor and I alone outdid him.

396
00:27:03,090 --> 00:27:06,150
And whenever we fought on the
Trojan plain with the bronze,

397
00:27:06,150 --> 00:27:08,650
he never lingered back in the mass of men or the crush,

398
00:27:08,650 --> 00:27:14,870
but ran far out in front, yielding his fury to no one,
and he cut down many men in the terrible fighting.

399
00:27:14,870 --> 00:27:19,530
All of them I could not tell through or name —
all the host he killed in the Argives' defense;

400
00:27:19,530 --> 00:27:23,250
but such a man was the son of Telephus
he brought down with the bronze,

401
00:27:23,250 --> 00:27:26,250
the hero Eurypylus — and around him his Ceteian

402
00:27:26,250 --> 00:27:30,270
companions in crowds were slain,
for the sake of a woman's gifts.

403
00:27:30,270 --> 00:27:33,930
He was the handsomest man I ever saw, after noble Memnon.

404
00:27:33,930 --> 00:27:40,130
And when we climbed down into the horse Epeius built —
we, the best of the Argives — and it all lay upon me,

405
00:27:40,130 --> 00:27:45,630
to throw open our close-packed ambush or to shut it,
then the other leaders and counselors of the Danaans

406
00:27:45,630 --> 00:27:51,690
kept wiping tears away, and each man's legs shook
under him; but him — never once did my eyes see

407
00:27:51,690 --> 00:27:58,090
his beautiful skin go pale, or catch him wiping a
tear from his cheeks. No: he begged me, over and over,

408
00:27:58,090 --> 00:28:01,330
to let him out of the horse,
and kept fingering his sword-hilt

409
00:28:01,330 --> 00:28:06,010
and his spear heavy with bronze,
with evil in his heart for the Trojans.

410
00:28:06,010 --> 00:28:08,830
And when we had sacked the steep city of Priam,

411
00:28:08,830 --> 00:28:12,110
he boarded his ship with his
share and a fine prize of honor —

412
00:28:12,110 --> 00:28:18,050
never touched by a thrown spear, never gashed
in the close work, the way it happens so often

413
00:28:18,050 --> 00:28:22,990
in war. For Ares rages blind, and makes no distinctions.'

414
00:28:22,990 --> 00:28:29,730
So I spoke; and the soul of swift-footed Aeacides went
away with long strides across the meadow of asphodel,

415
00:28:29,730 --> 00:28:32,710
rejoicing that I had said his son was preeminent.

416
00:28:32,710 --> 00:28:38,630
And the other souls of the dead who are gone stood
grieving, and each asked after what was dear to it.

417
00:28:38,630 --> 00:28:44,910
Alone of them all, the soul of Ajax, Telamon's son,
stood apart, still raging over the victory

418
00:28:44,910 --> 00:28:47,810
I won against him, pleading my case by the ships,

419
00:28:47,810 --> 00:28:51,890
for the arms of Achilles.
His honored mother set the prize,

420
00:28:51,890 --> 00:28:55,810
and the sons of the Trojans judged it, and Pallas Athena.

421
00:28:55,810 --> 00:28:58,830
Would to god I had never won a contest like that one:

422
00:28:58,830 --> 00:29:02,070
such a head the earth closed over for those arms' sake —

423
00:29:02,070 --> 00:29:05,410
Ajax, who in beauty and in every deed of war stood first

424
00:29:05,410 --> 00:29:09,310
of all the Danaans, after the blameless son of Peleus.

425
00:29:09,310 --> 00:29:11,410
Him I addressed with honeyed words:

426
00:29:11,410 --> 00:29:14,750
'Ajax, son of blameless Telamon — could you not, then,

427
00:29:14,750 --> 00:29:21,130
even in death forget your anger at me for those cursed arms?
The gods made them a scourge for the Argives —

428
00:29:21,130 --> 00:29:26,530
such a tower fell when you fell. For you the
Achaeans grieve without ceasing, as for the very head

429
00:29:26,530 --> 00:29:33,250
of Achilles, Peleus' son. And no one else bears the
blame, but Zeus: he hated the army of the Danaan

430
00:29:33,250 --> 00:29:36,630
spearmen with a terrible hatred,
and laid your doom on you.

431
00:29:36,630 --> 00:29:39,650
Come here, lord — come hear our word, our speech.

432
00:29:39,650 --> 00:29:42,950
Master your fury; break that proud heart down.'

433
00:29:42,950 --> 00:29:45,810
So I spoke. He answered me nothing. He went his way

434
00:29:45,810 --> 00:29:49,350
after the other souls, into the
Erebus of the dead who are gone.

435
00:29:49,350 --> 00:29:55,550
There, even so, for all his anger, he might yet have
spoken to me, or I to him. But the heart in my breast

436
00:29:55,550 --> 00:29:58,270
wished to see the souls of the other dead.

437
00:29:58,270 --> 00:30:06,550
There I saw Minos, the shining son of Zeus,
holding a golden scepter, delivering law among the dead —

438
00:30:06,550 --> 00:30:11,210
he seated, and around him the dead
pleading their cases before the lord,

439
00:30:11,210 --> 00:30:14,710
sitting and standing through
the wide-gated house of Hades.

440
00:30:14,710 --> 00:30:20,870
And after him I marked out huge Orion herding the
wild beasts together across the meadow of asphodel —

441
00:30:20,870 --> 00:30:27,300
the very beasts he had killed on the lonely mountains —
a club in his hands, all bronze, forever unbroken.

442
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:33,850
And I saw Tityus, the son of glorious Earth,
laid out on the ground: over nine roods he sprawled,

443
00:30:33,850 --> 00:30:37,430
and two vultures, sitting either side, tore at his liver,

444
00:30:37,430 --> 00:30:40,890
plunging into the caul;
and his hands could not beat them off:

445
00:30:40,890 --> 00:30:44,430
for he had assaulted Leto, the honored consort of Zeus,

446
00:30:44,430 --> 00:30:48,670
as she walked toward Pytho through
Panopeus of the lovely dancing-grounds.

447
00:30:48,670 --> 00:30:54,430
And Tantalus too I saw, holding his hard agonies,
standing in a pool that lapped against his chin —

448
00:30:54,430 --> 00:30:58,490
and he strained with thirst,
and could not reach a drop to drink:

449
00:30:58,490 --> 00:31:01,950
as often as the old man stooped, in a fury to drink,

450
00:31:01,950 --> 00:31:05,490
so often the water was swallowed
away and gone, and around his feet

451
00:31:05,490 --> 00:31:08,890
the black earth showed, parched dry by a god.

452
00:31:08,890 --> 00:31:12,230
And trees in tall leaf poured their fruit above his head,

453
00:31:12,230 --> 00:31:17,870
pears and pomegranates and apple trees bright with
fruit, sweet figs and olives in full flourish;

454
00:31:17,870 --> 00:31:21,010
and whenever the old man reached
out to close his hands on them,

455
00:31:21,010 --> 00:31:24,470
the wind would toss them away toward the shadowing clouds.

456
00:31:24,470 --> 00:31:30,530
And Sisyphus too I saw, holding his hard agonies,
wrestling a monstrous stone with both his arms.

457
00:31:30,530 --> 00:31:33,470
Bracing himself — straining with hands and feet —

458
00:31:33,470 --> 00:31:37,250
he would shove the stone uphill
toward the crest; but each time,

459
00:31:37,250 --> 00:31:41,310
just as he was about to heave it over the top,
the sheer weight wheeled it round,

460
00:31:41,310 --> 00:31:44,290
and down to the plain again rolled the shameless stone.

461
00:31:44,290 --> 00:31:50,470
And he would strain again, and shove — the sweat
streaming off his limbs, and the dust rising off his head.

462
00:31:50,470 --> 00:31:53,230
And after him I marked the strength of Heracles —

463
00:31:53,230 --> 00:31:56,830
his phantom: for the man himself,
among the deathless gods,

464
00:31:56,830 --> 00:32:00,270
delights in the feasting,
with fair-ankled Hebe for his wife,

465
00:32:00,270 --> 00:32:04,110
the child of great Zeus and of Hera of the golden sandals.

466
00:32:04,110 --> 00:32:07,390
Around that phantom rose a clamor of the dead, like birds

467
00:32:07,390 --> 00:32:14,230
scattering in panic every way. And he, like black night,
the bow bare in his hand, an arrow on the string,

468
00:32:14,230 --> 00:32:17,670
glared terribly about him, forever
like a man on the point of shooting.

469
00:32:17,670 --> 00:32:21,070
And terrible was the baldric crossing his chest,

470
00:32:21,070 --> 00:32:24,910
the sword-belt all of gold,
where deeds of wonder had been worked —

471
00:32:24,910 --> 00:32:28,610
bears, and wild boars, and lions with fire in their eyes,

472
00:32:28,610 --> 00:32:32,210
and combats, and battles,
and murders, and the killings of men.

473
00:32:32,210 --> 00:32:38,130
May the man who designed that belt design nothing
further — whoever took such work into his craft!

474
00:32:38,130 --> 00:32:44,370
And he in his turn knew me, the moment his eyes fell
on me, and mourning he spoke winged words to me:

475
00:32:44,370 --> 00:32:51,210
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —
poor man! So you too are dragging out an evil doom

476
00:32:51,210 --> 00:32:54,090
such as I bore beneath the rays of the sun?

477
00:32:54,090 --> 00:33:00,750
I was a son of Zeus, of Cronos' line — and my misery
had no end: for I was bound in service to a man

478
00:33:00,750 --> 00:33:07,230
far worse than I, who laid hard labors on me,
and once he sent me here, to fetch the Hound: no other

479
00:33:07,230 --> 00:33:09,930
labor, he reckoned, could be mightier than that one.

480
00:33:09,930 --> 00:33:16,010
I carried the Hound up and out of Hades,
and Hermes saw me on my way, and gray-eyed Athena.'

481
00:33:16,010 --> 00:33:18,670
So speaking he went back into the house of Hades;

482
00:33:18,670 --> 00:33:24,830
but I stayed rooted there, in hope some other might
yet come of the hero men who died in the time before.

483
00:33:24,830 --> 00:33:28,890
And now I would have seen still
earlier men, the ones I longed for —

484
00:33:28,890 --> 00:33:32,170
Theseus and Pirithous, glorious children of the gods —

485
00:33:32,170 --> 00:33:35,150
but first the tribes of the dead came gathering, thousand

486
00:33:35,150 --> 00:33:41,190
on thousand, with a sound that had no
earthly likeness; and the pale terror took me

487
00:33:41,190 --> 00:33:48,510
that queenly Persephone might send out of Hades
the Gorgon head of the dread monster against me.

488
00:33:48,510 --> 00:33:55,090
Straight to the ship I went, and ordered my companions
to board in their turn and loose the stern-cables;

489
00:33:55,090 --> 00:33:59,450
and they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks.

490
00:33:59,450 --> 00:34:03,170
And the wave of the current bore
her down the river of Ocean —

491
00:34:03,170 --> 00:34:06,888
the rowing first; and afterward,
a beautiful following wind.
